WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.436
Food supply concentration.

00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:04.556
The grocery oligopoly.

00:00:04.556 --> 00:00:15.613
Three companies control over sixty percent of Canada's grocery market: Loblaw Companies, owned by the Weston family, with brands including Loblaws, No Frills, Shoppers Drug Mart, and President's Choice.

00:00:15.613 --> 00:00:21.943
Sobeys, owned by Empire Company, with brands including Sobeys, Safeway, FreshCo, and IGA.

00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:26.409
And Metro, with brands including Metro, Food Basics, and Jean Coutu.

00:00:26.409 --> 00:00:35.829
In 2017, it was revealed that major Canadian bread producers and grocers had participated in a bread price-fixing conspiracy lasting over fourteen years.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:43.170
Loblaw admitted its participation, offered twenty-five-dollar gift cards to affected customers, and faced class action lawsuits.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:51.306
Galen Weston Junior, the chairman and controlling shareholder of Loblaw, was summoned to testify before parliamentary committees about grocery pricing.

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:58.090
The Grocery Code of Conduct was proposed to regulate supplier-grocer relationships but faced resistance from major grocers.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.624
Food inflation has outpaced wage growth for years.

00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:07.409
Canadians spend more of their income on food while the grocery oligopoly reports record profits.

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:10.647
Three companies that control Canada's food supply.

00:01:10.647 --> 00:01:13.249
The bread price-fixing conspiracy.

00:01:13.249 --> 00:01:19.227
For fourteen years, major Canadian bread producers and grocery companies coordinated bread prices.

00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:29.647
The conspiracy was first disclosed publicly in late 2017 when Loblaw Companies admitted its participation and applied for immunity under the Competition Bureau's leniency program.

00:01:29.647 --> 00:01:38.386
Loblaw offered twenty-five-dollar gift cards to affected customers, which consumer advocates described as inadequate given the fourteen-year duration of the conspiracy.

00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:42.306
Class action lawsuits were filed seeking substantial damages.

00:01:42.306 --> 00:01:46.124
The Competition Bureau investigation continued for years.

00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:48.488
The Grocery Code of Conduct.

00:01:48.488 --> 00:01:58.045
Proposed to regulate the relationship between grocery retailers and their suppliers — farmers, food producers, and smaller companies who depend on the big three for market access.

00:01:58.045 --> 00:02:03.693
The code would establish fair dealing standards, payment timelines, and dispute resolution mechanisms.

00:02:03.693 --> 00:02:06.715
The major grocers initially resisted the code.

00:02:06.715 --> 00:02:11.249
Loblaw was the last major holdout before eventually agreeing to participate.

00:02:11.249 --> 00:02:18.499
The code's effectiveness remains to be seen — voluntary codes in concentrated markets have historically produced limited outcomes.

00:02:18.499 --> 00:02:20.659
Food as captured market.

00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:26.568
The grocery oligopoly demonstrates institutional capture applied to the most basic human need: food.

00:02:26.568 --> 00:02:30.181
Three companies control over sixty percent of the market.

00:02:30.181 --> 00:02:33.272
They fixed bread prices for fourteen years.

00:02:33.272 --> 00:02:36.829
Their profits increased during the cost-of-living crisis.

00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:40.886
The Competition Bureau investigated but consequences were modest.

00:02:40.886 --> 00:02:43.318
The Grocery Code was resisted.

00:02:43.318 --> 00:02:47.545
The lobbying infrastructure maintains favourable policy outcomes.

00:02:47.545 --> 00:02:52.965
And every Canadian must eat every day — making food the most inescapable captured market.

00:02:52.965 --> 00:02:55.465
You can choose not to buy a house.

00:02:55.465 --> 00:02:58.079
You can choose not to visit a doctor.

00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:00.477
You cannot choose not to eat.

00:03:00.477 --> 00:03:06.102
When three companies control your food supply, every meal is a transaction with a captured market.

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:09.704
This is institutional capture at its most fundamental.
